\bottomrule
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^*$Permutation $p \leq 10^{-4}$ for all 1v2 comparisons shown (BH-corrected $q_{\max} = 0.003$).} \\
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Rewrite mode (ablation); excluded from continuation-mode $\Kempirical$ claim.} \\
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^\ddagger$Generator: Qwen2.5-7B (fp16); scorer: Qwen2.5-1.5B.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} and Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta}(a) show $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ for 7/9 continuation-mode C4 configurations across four of six tested architecture families (4 Qwen + 3 non-Qwen; 1v2 $\auc$ range: 0.6112--0.6539; all CI lower bounds $> \theta$; permutation $p \leq 10^{-4}$).
The 1v2 vs.\ 2v3 AUC gap ranges from 5.0 to 10.6\,pp across configurations, exhibiting a sharp cliff at the boundary: all 2v3 values fall below $\theta$ while 1v2 values remain 1--10\,pp above it.
Gemma-2-2B and Mistral-7B are competence-limited at $\Kempirical{=}1$ (1v2 $\auc$: 0.598, CI $[0.588, 0.608]$; and 0.594, respectively): both achieve near-perfect 0v1 separation but fail to sustain 1v2 above $\theta$.
Among the remaining non-Qwen families, Pythia and OLMo yield clear $\Kempirical{=}2$; LLaMA-3.1-8B is borderline (1v2 $\auc = 0.6123$, CI lower $= 0.603$).
An independent F1@0.50 analysis confirms the same boundary for all configurations (Appendix~\ref{sec:f1_validation}).
All four decoding strategies yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}), and $\Kempirical{=}2$ persists from 1.5B through 14B (Appendix~\ref{sec:boundary_extended}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_kstar_theta.pdf}
\caption{(a) Pairwise $\auc$ decay across depth pairs for model-mode configurations (solid) and decoding strategies (dashed); horizontal dashed line marks $\theta = 0.60$; AUC computed on held-out folds. (b) $\Kempirical(\theta)$ sensitivity: 7/9 continuation-mode configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Gemma-2-2B and Mistral-7B yield $\Kempirical{=}1$); at $\theta{=}0.70$, all drop to $\Kempirical{=}1$.}
\label{fig:kstar_theta}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta} shows $\Kempirical$ as a function of $\theta$: web text $\Kempirical{=}2$ is stable across $\theta \in [0.575, 0.625]$, with transitions to $\Kempirical{=}3$ at $\theta{=}0.55$ (Qwen-rewrite) and $\Kempirical{=}1$ at $\theta{=}0.70$; the domain ordering is preserved across the full range (Table~\ref{tab:theta_sensitivity}).
A changepoint analysis (PELT~\cite{killick2012optimal}) provides complementary, threshold-free evidence: exact agreement is 18/37 (49\%; linear weighted $\kappa{=}0.42$, moderate agreement), rising to 35/37 (95\%) within $\pm 1$; PELT is systematically conservative ($\Kempirical_{\text{PELT}} \leq \Kempirical_{\text{OBD}}$ in all 19 disagreements; Appendix~\ref{sec:changepoint_validation}).
We do not rely on changepoint agreement for $\Kempirical$ determination.

\paragraph{Margin stratification.}
To assess the robustness of individual $\Kempirical{=}2$ assignments, we stratify the seven continuation-mode configurations by their margin above $\theta$ (CI lower $-$ $\theta$; Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}).
Three configurations across three families show confident margins exceeding 1\,pp: Pythia-1.4B (4.5\,pp), Qwen-base (1.7\,pp), and Qwen-14B (1.2\,pp).
Four have marginal support (${<}\,1$\,pp): Qwen-7B (0.8\,pp), OLMo-1B (0.5\,pp), LLaMA-8B (0.3\,pp), and Qwen-cont (0.2\,pp); the last drops to $\Kempirical{=}1$ under cross-scorer control (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).
We emphasize that $\Kempirical$ is determined by bootstrap CI (Eq.~\ref{eq:kstar}), not by permutation $p$-values, which serve as supplementary validation.
The $\theta$-invariant domain ordering (Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}) is supported by the confident configurations alone and does not depend on marginal cases.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer and Its Limits}
\label{sec:transfer}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_transfer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model transfer heatmap. Each cell shows 1v2 boundary $\auc$ when trained on the row model and tested on the column model. Diagonal entries (bold) are in-domain; off-diagonal entries show transfer performance. Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo transfer retains $\auc > \theta$ (0.590--0.599); Qwen-rewrite transfers above $\theta$ to Qwen-cont (0.619) but not to Pythia/OLMo.}
\label{fig:transfer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Aggregate transfer is strong: continuation-mode classifiers retain 80--92\% ordinal accuracy across generators at $\leq$1.5B (Table~\ref{tab:s1_transfer}), and 96--100\% mean pairwise AUC at 7B (Appendix~\ref{sec:7b_transfer}).
However, boundary-level transfer is fragile: at 1.5B, most off-diagonal 1v2 $\auc$ values fall below $\theta$ (Figure~\ref{fig:transfer_heatmap}); at 7B, 4/6 off-diagonal 1v2 pairs likewise fall below $\theta$, so $\Kempirical{=}2$ assignments do not transfer at the boundary even as overall discriminability improves with scale.
Qwen-rewrite classifiers transfer poorly (61--63\%), consistent with reliance on mode-specific artifacts.

\paragraph{Cross-scorer validation.}
Replacing the self-scorer with Pythia-1.4B or OLMo-1B preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$; Table~\ref{tab:crossscorer}) and $\Kempirical$ in 20/22 cross-scorer configurations on C4 and arXiv; the two exceptions are the most marginal boundary configurations (AUC within 0.002 of $\theta$).
Notably, the most marginal self-scored configuration (Qwen-cont, CI lower $= 0.602$, margin $0.2$\,pp) drops to $\Kempirical{=}1$ under Pythia and OLMo scoring (1v2 AUC $= 0.592$/$0.586$), reducing the continuation-mode count from 7/9 to 6/9 under strict cross-scorer control.
For arXiv, Pythia-1.4B cross-scoring preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ for 3/4 generators (2v3 $\auc$: 0.608--0.632); Qwen-7B drops to borderline $\Kempirical{=}2$ ($\Delta = -2.7$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}).
A three-way ANOVA confirms Depth-Pair dominates variance ($\eta^2_p = 0.998$; Scorer: $\eta^2_p = 0.192$; Table~\ref{tab:anova_crossscorer}); replacing the 1.5B scorer with Qwen2.5-7B preserves $\Kempirical$ identically ($\Delta\,\auc < 1$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:scorer_scale}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Feature Ordering and Generation Mode}
\label{sec:feature_degradation}
\label{sec:generation_mode}

Among continuation-mode models, single-feature $\auc$ rankings are highly correlated ($\rho \geq 0.85$; Table~\ref{tab:top_features}), with the top-3 (\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}, \texttt{surp\_std}, \texttt{surp\_d2\_std}) shared across families; no single subset of size ${<}8$ sustains $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_subset}), and surprisal dominates on arXiv while C4 relies on feature combinations (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_ablation}).
Qwen-rewrite diverges ($\Delta = +8.4$\,pp), consistent with mode-specific artifacts; continuation mode is the canonical condition.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Neural Probe}
\label{sec:neural_probe}

A neural MLP probe on frozen encoder states (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}) raises $K^*_{\text{neural}}$ to 3 for Qwen (2v3 $\auc = 0.616$) but not Pythia or OLMo ($\auc_{2v3}$: 0.596, 0.589), reflecting self-familiarity artifacts; the 15D statistical features yield consistent $\Kempirical = 2$ across all families.

\paragraph{Fine-tuned DeBERTa upper bound.}
DeBERTa-v3-base ordinal classifiers raise 1v2 $\auc$ over LightGBM at all three scales (1.5B: $0.705$ vs.\ $0.626$; 14B: $0.678$ vs.\ $0.621$; 32B: $0.694$ vs.\ $0.618$) yet preserve $\Kempirical{=}2$ (all 2v3 CI lower ${<}\,\theta$; Table~\ref{tab:deberta_comparison}); LightGBM shifts by ${<}\,0.5$\,pp across 1.5B--32B.
The classifier gap reveals a feature-set-dependent two-tier structure: zero on C4 and Code (DeBERTa $\Kempirical{=}2$, matching OBD), but $+2$ on Wikipedia (DeBERTa $\Kempirical{=}3$; OLMo excluded) and $+1$ on arXiv (DeBERTa $\Kempirical{=}4$, 3v4 $\auc = 0.624$; Table~\ref{tab:deberta_arxiv}); cross-transfer on Wikipedia mirrors the OBD pattern ($\Kempirical{=}3$ drops to ${\sim}2$; Table~\ref{tab:wiki_deberta_transfer}).
The C4 result ($\Kempirical_{\text{DeBERTa}} = \Kempirical_{\text{OBD}} = 2$) establishes a representation-independent boundary: even a 184M-parameter fine-tuned encoder cannot surpass $\Kempirical{=}2$ on web text.
The neural probe (Section~\ref{sec:neural_probe}), which uses frozen encoder representations with a linear head, provides an intermediate reference point between 15D statistics and full DeBERTa fine-tuning.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{DeBERTa-v3-base vs.\ LightGBM on C4 generations at three scales. Both classifiers yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at 1.5B, 14B, and 32B.}
\label{tab:deberta_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
& \textbf{0v1} & \textbf{1v2} & \textbf{2v3} & \textbf{3v4} & \textbf{4v5} \\
\midrule
LightGBM (1.5B) & ---  & .626 & .549 & ---  & --- \\
DeBERTa (1.5B)  & .992 & .705 & .599 & ---  & --- \\
\midrule
LightGBM (14B)  & .960 & .621 & .549 & .524 & --- \\
DeBERTa (14B)   & .994 & .678 & .590 & .565 & --- \\
\midrule
LightGBM (32B)  & .958 & .618 & .545 & .522 & .516 \\
DeBERTa (32B)   & .993 & .694 & .594 & .567 & .543 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Cross-Domain Variation}
\label{sec:cross_domain}

To test whether $\Kempirical$ reflects domain structure or model identity, we extend evaluation to arXiv ($n{=}2{,}200$), Wikipedia ($n{=}5{,}000$), and Code ($n{=}5{,}000$) across six configurations spanning four families (Table~\ref{tab:domain_kstar}).

\paragraph{arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$): five-model cross-validation.}
All five domain-competent models yield $\Kempirical{=}3$ (2v3 $\auc$: 0.6215--0.6545, all ${>}\,\theta$), reflecting formulaic features (LaTeX formatting, citation patterns) that degrade slowly and supply persistent depth signal across four families (1.4B--8B).
Removing LaTeX markup reduces 2v3 AUC by ${\sim}$1.8\,pp but preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ (CI lower $= 0.610$; Table~\ref{tab:latex_ablation}).
OLMo-1B is a capacity failure (0v1 JSD $= 0.33$), not a counter-example; Mistral-7B provides the strongest domain-dependence evidence ($\Kempirical{=}1$ on C4 but $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv).
The arXiv subset ($n{=}2{,}200$ per depth) is smaller than C4 ($n{=}5{,}000$); at equal sample size, wider bootstrap CIs shift borderline C4 configurations below $\theta$, yielding $\Kempirical{=}1$; the $\Kempirical{=}2$ verdict for these marginal C4 cases requires ${\geq}5{,}000$ samples.

\paragraph{Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$): single-generation saturation.}
With cross-family scorer control, all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ (1v2 $\auc$: 0.498--0.595, all below $\theta$): encyclopedic regularity provides minimal signal beyond the 0v1 boundary.
Same-family scoring inflates Qwen to $\Kempirical{=}2$ ($\Delta = +2.4$\,pp); cross-family scoring controls this bias (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}).

\paragraph{Code ($\Kempirical{=}1$--$2$): cross-modality confirmation.}
Python functions from CodeSearchNet~\cite{husain2019codesearchnet} ($n{=}5{,}000$) yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ for four models (1v2 $\auc$: 0.615--0.739; Table~\ref{tab:code_crossmodel_kstar}) with the same sharp 2v3 collapse as C4 (JSD ratio $11.7$--$13.5\times$); Pythia-1.4B is capacity-limited ($\Kempirical{=}1$, $\auc = 0.598$; cross-family scorer: $\Delta \leq 1.9$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:code_domain}).

\paragraph{Multi-source generator mixing.}
Multi-source pooling (three generators per domain; Appendix~\ref{sec:multi_source_details}) preserves domain-calibrated $\Kempirical$ across all four domains (C4 $\Kempirical{=}2$, unified 1v2 $\auc = 0.711$, generator identity $+0.010$ mean $\auc$ [range: $+0.001$, $+0.022$]; $\Kempirical$ unchanged in all eight configurations; arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$; Code $\Kempirical{=}2$; Wikipedia $\Kempirical{=}1$), independently confirming the cross-scorer correction on Wikipedia.

\noindent\begin{minipage}{\columnwidth}
\centering
\footnotesize
\captionof{table}{Domain $\times$ Model $\Kempirical$ matrix (scorer-controlled). Wikipedia values use cross-family scorer (Pythia-1.4B). $^*$OLMo arXiv: capacity failure. $^\dagger$Same-family scorer yields $\Kempirical{=}2$; controlled value shown. $^\ddagger$Borderline: Pythia CI upper $= 0.603$, Mistral CI lower $= 0.603$.}
\label{tab:domain_kstar}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{C4} & \textbf{Code} & \textbf{arXiv} & \textbf{Wikipedia} \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B    & 2 & 2 & 3 & 1$^\dagger$ \\
Qwen-7B      & 2 & 2 & 3 & 1$^\dagger$ \\
Pythia-1.4B  & 2 & 1$^\ddagger$ & 3 & 1 \\
OLMo-1B      & 2 & --- & 1$^*$ & 1 \\
Gemma-2-2B   & 1 & --- & --- & --- \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & 2 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\
Mistral-7B   & 1 & 2$^\ddagger$ & 3 & 1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{minipage}

\paragraph{Summary.}
\label{sec:binary_confidence}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ for web text holds across four of six families (1B--14B), four decoding strategies, and multi-source pools (Gemma-2-2B and Mistral-7B: $\Kempirical{=}1$; binary detectors: $K^*_{\text{binary}} \leq 1$, Table~\ref{tab:s9_binary_spearman}).
The domain ordering arXiv $>$ C4 $=$ Code $>$ Wikipedia is $\theta$-invariant; scale and architecture do not alter this ordering, confirming structural complexity as the primary determinant.

