\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 7 continuation-mode C4 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{=}7$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ reflects concentrated information loss: starting from first-generation text ($\auc_{0v1} \geq 0.92$), a single additional generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper content (1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp; 2v3 falls below $\theta$).

\paragraph{Operational value of the precise boundary.}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ resolves the question of filtering granularity: graduated weighting beyond depth~2 offers no advantage (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}), and the $\theta$-invariant domain ordering extends this across domains (Table~\ref{tab:deberta_arxiv}; rate sweeps in Appendix~\ref{sec:rate_sweep}).

\paragraph{Prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain.}
All main results use prompt-chain ($\Kempirical{=}2$, text-intrinsic information loss).
Retrain-chain yields $\Kempirical{=}7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:retrain_chain}), bounding the range; whether prompt-chain laundering masks retrain-chain signals remains open.

\paragraph{JSD decay and domain dependence.}
Token-level JSD reveals two decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 exhibits a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.9$), arXiv decays $3\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$), and the retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$; Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).
Domain determines the $\Kempirical$ ordering; Wikipedia's template-driven structure causes single-generation saturation ($\Kempirical{=}1$; Table~\ref{tab:domain-ttr-entropy}).

\paragraph{Feature-space ceiling and $\Kempirical_{\text{15D}}$ vs.\ $\Kempirical_{\text{intrinsic}}$.}
Absolute $\Kempirical$ values are \emph{feature-dependent}: DeBERTa extends $\Kempirical$ by $+2$ on Wikipedia and $+1$ on arXiv (Tables~\ref{tab:wiki_deberta_comparison}, \ref{tab:deberta_arxiv}), but matches the 15D bound on C4 and Code ($\Kempirical{=}2$), establishing a \emph{representation-independent boundary} on web text (Table~\ref{tab:deberta_filtering}).
The progression 15D $\to$ neural probes ($K^*_{\text{neural}}{=}3$) $\to$ DeBERTa ($\Kempirical{=}4$) shows diminishing returns, suggesting $K^*_\text{theory} \approx 3$--$4$; permutation importance confirms saturation at 2v3 (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_importance}).

\paragraph{Multi-source pooling and transfer.}
Multi-source pooling preserves domain-calibrated $\Kempirical$ across all four domains, partially addressing the 4/6 off-diagonal boundary-level failure at 7B (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}): domain-calibrated ceilings hold under mixed-generator training even though $\Kempirical{=}2$ cannot be assumed when the generator is unknown.

